1795 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Black Women's March for Reparations and Well-living \(buen vivir\) is a nationwide mobilization that brings together Black women from across Brazil in the fight against racism, sexism, and other structural inequalities that intersect and shape our lives and territories. In 2025, we return to the streets with another central and urgent demand: Reproductive Justice, an expression of the autonomy, self-determination, and dignity in our sexual and reproductive lives that we have been claiming for centuries.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1578 0 R
/Next 1913 0 R
>>
endobj
1796 0 obj
<<
/Title (More than the right to choose, Reproductive Justice affirms that reproductive freedom is only possible when the conditions for a life of dignity are fully realized. In this sense, Reproductive Justice is inseparable from Social Justice. This framework encompasses all people affected by reproductive inequities, including Black women, people who can become pregnant, trans men, travestis, people with disabilities, and people living with HIV/AIDS.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1578 0 R
/Prev 1913 0 R
>>
endobj
1797 0 obj
<<
/Title (In line with international movements that advocate a pleasure-centered and affirmative view of sexuality, we reaffirm that sexual and reproductive health should be experienced with pleasure, care, and respect, never under guilt, control, or fear.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1578 0 R
/Next 1914 0 R
>>
endobj
1798 0 obj
<<
/Title (6.3. PROMOTE INCLUSIVE SEXUAL AND REPRODUCTIVE HEALTH)
/Dest [36 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1915 0 R
/Next 1579 0 R
>>
